## 1. INTRODUCTION
The rational synthesis of nanomaterials with specific morphologies, band gaps, defects, and spatial architectures is of great importance due to the intriguing properties and widespread applications of the materials in energy conversion, electronics, photonics, and many other fields.1,2 To date, significant progress has been made in this growing research field, and various synthetic strategies have been developed. Particularly, the transformation strategy is capable of producing diverse nanomaterials with well-defined structures and fascinating performance, which cannot be easily prepared by other methods.3−7 Specifically, physical and/or chemical processes transform pre-existing precursors into the target materials. However, conventional transformation strategies often encounter difficulties in simultaneously manipulating multiple parameters of the target materials. The synergetic transformation strategy has been regarded as an efficient technique for this purpose, simultaneously controlling different structural parameters.8,9 In sharp contrast to the conventional transformation strategy, which adopts single physical/chemical tool, this new strategy simultaneously uses various transformation tools. The key to a synergetic transformation strategy lies in the cooperation and/or competition among different transformation tools through dynamics and/or thermodynamics. Moreover, inorganic−organic hybrids have emerged as efficient precursors for transformation in the past decade because the nano/molecule/atom-scale distributed inorganic and organic species can serve as self-templates and sacrificial agents, respectively.10,11 However, the present inorganic−organic hybrid precursors are mainly limited to high-cost porous coordination polymers, such as metal− organic frameworks (MOFs).12−14 Therefore, the synergetic transformation of solid and low-cost inorganic−organic hybrids to produce advanced nanomaterials with novel and complicated structures is of great interest.
Photo(electro)/electrocatalytic (i.e., photocatalytic, electrocatalytic, and photoelectrocatalytic) hydrogen evolution from water splitting has been considered as a promising method to solve the global energy crisis and environmental pollution.15−17 At present, the energy conversion efficiency is still limited by the complexity of the photo(electro)/electrocatalytic system.
For example, photocatalytic water splitting includes the following steps: photon adsorption, charge separation and transfer, and surface reactions, which require proper band gaps, shortened transfer paths and abundant active sites, respectively.18,19 The synergetic transformation of solid inorganic− organic hybrids theoretically offers a promising toolbox for synthesizing advanced nanomaterials that meet the complicated requirements and can efficiently optimize every intermediate step in photo(electro)/electrocatalytic water splitting.
Herein, we summarize recent progress in the synergetic transformation of solid inorganic−organic hybrids toward the production of advanced nanomaterials and the applications of these materials in photo(electro)/electrocatalytic water splitting. Various functional nanomaterials have been prepared based on different synergetic transformation strategies. Importantly, we intend to reveal the synergetic transformation mechanism based on understanding the cooperation and/or competition among different transformation tools.
## 2. SYNTHETIC APPROACHES
Diverse synergetic transformation strategies have been developed to transform solid inorganic−organic hybrids into advanced nanomaterials. Herein, the solid inorganic−organic hybrids originate from synthetic nanostructures by low-cost and scalable solvothermal methods. The synergetic transformation strategies are categorized by organics removal coupled with different methods of reconstructing inorganic components, including ion exchange, interfacial reaction, redox reaction, and self-assembly.
## 2.1. Coupling Ion Exchange with Organics Removal
Ion exchange has been demonstrated as an efficient method to transform pre-existing nanomaterials into target products.20,21 However, the conventional ion exchange reaction usually produces nanomaterials with the inherited morphology, dimensions, and size of their parental precursors. Manufacturing novel structures via only ion exchange of inorganic nanomaterials is highly challenging. To solve this problem, solid inorganic−organic hybrids are developed as precursors, and organics removal is introduced in combination with ion exchange. As a typical example for controlled transformation, porous nanosheets, nanoframes and branched nanowire arrays have been successfully synthesized from inorganic−organic hybrid nanosheets through the coupling of ion exchange and organics removal (Figure 1a).8,22−24 The key lies in velocity matching between ion exchange and organics removal, which can be modulated by adjusting the amounts of the stabilizing agent diethylenetriamine (DETA). When the ZnS−DETA hybrid nanosheets obtained after purification by washing encounter $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions, the rapid ion exchange kinetics induce the formation of a $\mathrm{Cd}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}S$ nanosheet-based skeleton. At the same time, the dissolution of DETA into the solvent generates pores in the nanosheets. As a result, porous $\mathrm{Cd}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}S$ nanosheets are prepared via the coupling of rapid ion exchange and organics removal. The rapid ion-exchange-induced retention of the nanosheets is further confirmed by the formation of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ nanoparticles when the $\mathrm{ZnS-DETA}$ precursors are hydrothermally treated without the addition of $\bar{\mathrm{Cd}}^{2+}$ ions.8 Interestingly, $\mathrm{Cd}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{Se}$ nanoframes are prepared through hydrothermal treatment on unpurified ZnSe−DETA nanosheets with $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions (Figure 1b). For unpurified $\mathrm{ZnSe-}$ DETA nanosheets (Figure 1c), numerous DETA molecules are adsorbed onto the basal plane of the ZnSe−DETA nanosheets rather than on the edges. Thus, rapid ion exchange proceeds in the edge regions to form $\mathrm{Cd}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-{\boldsymbol{x}}}\mathrm{Se}$ (Figure 1d), while the basal plane of the sheets begins to break into particles owing to the dissolution of DETA in water (Figure 1e). These broken nanoparticles dissolve and regrow on the edges through the typical Ostwald ripening mechanism to form $\mathrm{Cd}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{Se}$ nanoframes with branchlike nanorods (Figure 1f).22 Notably, when the amount of DETA in the aqueous $Z\mathrm{n}S$ −DETA nanosheet solution is continuously increased, the basal plane and edges become covered with DETA. The rate of ion exchange slows down, and the dissolution of DETA from hybrids into solution plays a dominant role. First, CdS nanoparticle arrays with regular spacing appear on the surfaces and edges of the $Z\mathrm{n}S$ −DETA hybrid nanosheets owing to the symmetric distribution of adsorbed DETA. Then, the other smooth parts are broken into pieces due to the dissolution of
DETA in water and the blocking of ion exchange. Meanwhile, $S^{2-}$ ions in the smooth regions of the hybrid nanosheets diffuse toward neighboring CdS nanoseeds to act as a sulfur source for further growth of the CdS branch. Finally, CdS branched nanowire arrays are obtained.23
In addition to modulating the rate of ion exchange, the intrinsic crystal structure can also be used to synthesize welldefined nanomaterials. As a representative example, hierarchical $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheet-based nanotubes were synthesized by combining the ion exchange of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ −EDA (EDA: ethylenediamine) hybrid nanowires with $\mathrm{~L~}$ -cysteine and organics removal.25 The $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ −EDA hybrid nanowires possess a smooth surface (Figure $^{2\mathrm{a,d}}$ ). After reaction with the sulfur source of L-cysteine at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ , the outer surface
