becomes vertically standing $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets due to the twodimensional crystal structure of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ .26,27 Meanwhile, the internal part of the intermediates remains a solid hybrid induced by the limited inward diffusion of $S^{2-}$ ions and outward diffusion of EDA (Figure 2b,e). With the increase in reaction time, the inner solid $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ −EDA hybrids continuously dissolve, and the outer $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets continue growing, owing to the stable difference between $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ −EDA and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ . Finally, hierarchical $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheet-based nanotubes with hollow structures are obtained (Figure 2c,f). Therefore, novel nanomaterials with hierarchical structures can be efficiently prepared because of the intrinsic crystal structure of the ion exchange products coupled with organics removal (Figure 2g).
## 2.2. Coupling Interfacial Reaction with Organics Removal
An interfacial reaction forms an inorganic phase on the shell, which is different from the inner hybrids. The shell will exert an effect on the subsequent removal of organic components from the inner hybrids. By matching the rates of the interfacial reaction and organics removal, various porous materials with/ without hollow structures can be obtained.28,29 For example, porous $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ perovskite nanowires were prepared through a rapid interfacial reaction coupled with organics removal (Figure 3a).28 As is known, the reaction between $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions and $\mathrm{HBr/CH_{3}N H_{3}B r}$ is so fast that the controlled synthesis of organolead halide perovskite nanomaterials is difficult. To solve this problem, inorganic−organic hybrid Pbcontaining $\mathrm{HSCH_{2}C H(N H_{2})C O O-P b-O H}$ nanowires are adopted as precursors (Figure 3b). After the rapid addition of hybrid $\mathrm{Pb}$ -containing nanowires into excess HBr and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Br},$ a thin layer of porous $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ formed on the surface of nanowires within a second (Figure 3c). The quickly formed porous $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ shell serves as protective layer to prevent the collapse of the overall nanostructure. Then, the slow outside-to-inside transformation of the inner $\mathrm{Pb}$ -containing nanowires produces porous $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ nanowires (Figure 3d).
Additionally, a hollow structure in the final products will be found when the rate of outward diffusion is faster than the interfacial reaction. One example is the synthesis of ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ mesoporous nanotubes by using this facile method (Figure 4e).29 Co−Asp (Asp: aspartic acid) nanowires were synthesized as precursors (Figure $\mathrm{4a,f,}$ ). When Co−Asp nanowires are annealed in air at $200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ a thin $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ layer with abundant pores is formed on the surface (Figure 4b). Meanwhile, the asformed thin $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ shell serves as an interface to allow the outward diffusion of inner Co−Asp induced by the inward diffusion of oxygen in air, leading to the appearance of a hollow interior (Figure $^{4}\mathrm{g})$ .30 When the reaction temperature increases to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ double-shelled $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-CoO}$ nanotubes are obtained resulting from the combination of organics decomposition and outward diffusion of interior $\mathrm{Co-Asp}$ hybrids in an oxygen-deficient environment (Figure $^{4\mathrm{c},\mathrm{h}}.$ ).31 In the following transformation, the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ shell blocks the oxidation of inner CoO, resulting in the outward diffusion of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions. As a result, prolonged thermal oxidization at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can ultimately lead to the formation of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ mesoporous nanotubes (Figure $\mathrm{4d,i}$ ).
## 2.3. Coupling Redox Reaction with Organics Removal
Unlike the adoption of an external oxidizing/reducing agent in conventional chemical transformation, the product of the decomposition of organics can be used in situ as an oxidizing/ reducing agent in synergetic transformation strategies.32,33 More importantly, carbon species can also be integrated in situ into materials via this strategy to increase the conductivity of the materials. A representative work is the synthesis of metallic $\mathrm{WO}_{3-\mathrm{x}}.$ −carbon porous nanorods (Figure 5a).32 After annealing treatment of the ${\mathrm{WO}}_{3}$ −EDA hybrid nanorods in argon at 700 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ EDA is quickly decomposed and released to generate a porous structure (Figure 5b). Meanwhile, the reductive atmosphere after thermal decomposition could extract oxygen atoms in ${\mathrm{WO}}_{3}$ in situ to produce oxygen vacancies. Compared with semiconductive $\mathrm{WO}_{3},$ the as-obtained $\mathrm{WO}_{3-\mathrm{x}}$ with many oxygen vacancies shows metallic properties, as confirmed by the projected density of states (Figure 5c). Moreover, some carbon atoms in EDA are converted into closely attached carbon species that can serve as rigid substrates to prevent nanoparticles from aggregation and improve electron transfer (Figure 5d,e). Therefore, the porous structure, sufficient oxygen vacancies and conductive carbon layer are formed in the target nanomaterials through a facile redox reaction combined with organics removal.
## 2.4. Coupling Self-Assembly with Organics Removal
After coupling self-assembly and organics removal, solid inorganic−organic hybrids can be converted into advanced nanomaterials with hierarchical structures.34,35 For example, urchin-like InS−TETA (TETA: triethylenetetramine) was transformed into hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ nanosheet-based hollow microspheres (Figure 6a).34 First, an inorganic−organic hybrid InS-TETA urchin composed of spherical inner parts and branch-like outer parts is synthesized (Figure 6b). Upon hydrothermal treatment on InS−TETA at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}_{\cdot}$ , the dissolution of TETA leads to the collapse of branches into InS nanoparticles, which will be deposited on the surface of the inner sphere because the required supersaturation on the hybrid sphere is lower than that in solution (Figure 6c). Meanwhile, dissolved TETA acts as capping ligand, inducing the anisotropic growth of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ into ultrathin nanosheets. Then, the spherical inner hybrids further dissolve and regrow to form hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ microspheres with void inner parts and nanosheet-based outer parts (Figure 6d). In addition to simultaneous self-assembly and organics removal, the selfassembly procedure can also occur after organics removal to form hierarchical structures. A typical example is the conversion synthesis of hierarchical ZnSe nanosheet-based microspheres from inorganic−organic hybrid ZnSe−DETA nanosheets (Figure $^{6\mathrm{e},\mathrm{g}}$ ).35 Time-dependent analysis identifies an obvious intermediate composed of crossed nanosheets with many nanoparticles on the surface (Figure 6f). These ultrathin nanosheets and nanoparticles generated from the release of DETA between the layers of the ZnSe and the collapse of ZnSe nanosheets, respectively, assemble into flower-like structures driven by the surface energy.36,37 Finally, Ostwald ripening turns the rough crossed nanosheets into hierarchical ZnSe nanosheet-based microspheres. Therefore, hierarchically structured nanomaterials with solid/hollow interiors can be controllably obtained through the rate competition between organics removal and self-assembly. When organics removal and self-assembly proceed at the same time, the hollow structure will be produced in the assembled structure (Figure $6\mathrm{a-d)}$ . Furthermore, when organics removal is very fast, the assembly of the exfoliated inorganics leads to the formation of a solid construction (Figure $_{6\mathrm{e-g}})$ ).
## 3. CATALYTIC WATER SPLITTING
A major challenge in sustainable energy conversion is the development of efficient photo(electro)/electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER). Advanced nanomaterials derived from the synergetic transformation of solid inorganic−organic hybrids have demonstrated the promising potential to meet the complicate requirements for photo(electro)/electrocatalytic water splitting.
## 3.1. Photo(electro)catalytic Water Splitting
The photo(electro)catalytic water splitting reaction involves three steps: (i) absorption of incident light over semiconductors to generate electron−hole pairs, (ii) charge separation and migration to the surface of semiconductors, and (iii) surface reactions for water reduction/oxidation.18,19 The overall efficiency is determined by the above three sequential steps. Materials engineering can benefit absorption over a broader spectrum (step i), improve charge separation and migration (step ii), and increase the number of active sites (step iii). In this regard, using a synergetic transformation strategy to improve the photo(electro)catalytic efficiency is a promising solution. For example, Yu et al. investigated the photocatalytic activity of single-crystal-like $\mathrm{Zn}_{{\boldsymbol{x}}}\mathrm{Cd}_{1-{\boldsymbol{x}}}\mathrm{S}$ porous nanosheets.8 As shown in Figure 7a, the band gap of singlecrystal-like $\mathrm{Zn}_{{\boldsymbol{x}}}\mathrm{Cd}_{1-{\boldsymbol{x}}}\mathrm{S}$ porous nanosheets can be easily adjusted through controlling the composition. UV−vis diffuse reflectance spectra show that the adsorption spectrum of $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ porous nanosheets shifts to longer wavelengths compared to that of $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ nanorods, confirming a decrease in the band gap (Figure 7b). A single-crystal-like structure can efficiently reduce the number of defects in $\mathrm{Zn}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{Cd}_{1-{\boldsymbol{x}}}\boldsymbol{S}$ porous nanosheets and suppress the combination of photogenerated electrons and holes. Furthermore, porous structures not only provide a large surface area but also lower the migration distance of photogenerated carriers.38 $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ porous nanosheets show a higher photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution performance than $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ nanorods (Figure 7c) because $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ porous nanosheets possess a favorable band structure, together with excellent charge mobility and plentiful active centers.18 Moreover, hierarchically structured CdS branched nanowire arrays, which are obtained by synergetic transformation (Figure 7d), show a much higher photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate than CdS nanoparticles and
CdS microdendrites (Figure 7e) for three reasons. The hierarchical structure facilitates the absorption of incident light. The single-crystal structure of every branch can reduce the number of defects.39 The ultrathin nanowires not only provide large surface areas, but also shorten charge migration distance. Notably, size-adjustable CdS branched nanowire arrays provide a chance to study and understand the sizedependent photocatalytic performance (Figure 7f). More importantly, the synergetic transformation product can be further processed into a photoelectrocatalyst,40 such as the selfsupported gradient oxygen-doped 3D CdS branched nanorod array (grad-O CdS), in which a continuous built-in band bending structure was constructed (Figure $\mathrm{7g}$ ).40 As shown in Figure $\mathrm{7h}$ , the efficiency of the charge separation $(\Phi_{\mathrm{sep}})$ of grad-O CdS reaches 0.86 at $0.4\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE, which is remarkably improved compared to that of pristine CdS ( $\ensuremath{\mathbf{\tilde{\Phi}}}_{\mathrm{sep}}=0.26)$ . The excellent stability of grad-O CdS is demonstrated by the photocurrent−time $\left(J-t\right)$ curve at a potential of $0.4\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE (Figure 7i and its inset). The enhanced activity and durability arise from the combination of the hierarchical nanowire array structure and continuous built-in band bending, which can promote photogenerated charge transportation in a one-way direction (Figure $\mathrm{7g}$ .
## 3.2. Electrocatalytic Water Splitting
The major challenge of electrocatalytic water splitting is the design and preparation of highly efficient electrocatalysts, which meet the requirements of high intrinsic activity, high conductivity, and abundant active sites.41−45 Nanomaterials, especially those with defined morphologies, defects, and spatial architectures, are highly favorable for electrocatalytic reactions. In this regard, great efforts have been made to design and prepare advanced electrocatalysts via a synergetic transformation strategy.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS AND PROSPECTS
Herein, we summarize recent advances in the synthesis of advanced nanomaterials through the synergetic transformation of solid inorganic−organic hybrids and the application of these materials in photo(electro)/electrocatalytic water splitting. Based on the tools for the reconstruction of inorganic components, the synergetic transformation strategies are classified as ion exchange, interfacial reaction, redox reaction and self-assembly coupled with organics removal. Owing to the wide availability of hybrid precursors and abundant transformation tools, the synergetic transformation strategy is capable of producing diverse advanced nanomaterials with unusual and complicated structures. The synergetic transformation products exhibit excellent performance for photo(electro)/electrocatalytic water splitting owing to the improved photon adsorption, facilitated electron transport, increased active sites, and enhanced intrinsic activity. Beyond the great progress made to date, there are considerable challenges and opportunities remaining for continued investigation in this area. (1) Novel nanomaterials are urgently required in the future. In this regard, the molecular design of novel solid inorganic−organic hybrid precursors is of great importance. Moreover, the existing synergetic transformation strategies need to be improved to precisely control the daughter materials. At the same time, the exploration of novel synergetic transformation strategies is highly desirable, especially for the preparation of in-plane heterogeneous and/ or atom-thick target catalysts. (2) The synergetic transformation mechanism of solid inorganic−organic hybrids toward advanced nanomaterials should be investigated through the introduction of various novel in situ characterization tools.46 Following this, the relationship between the properties and structures of nanomaterials derived from synergetic transformation can be systematically investigated. (3) For research using solid hybrids as precursors for synergetic transformation, a deep understanding of the interfacial interactions among different components is crucial since the interface has a great effect on the following catalytic applications. (4) The current synergetic transformation products generally originate from monometal-based hybrids with a single active component, which hinders further improvement of their catalytic performance. In this regard, double/triple metal-based inorganic−organic hybrids with a rational distribution can be designed and adopted as templates. With multidisciplinary efforts from physics, chemistry, materials science, and chemical engineering, the advanced synergetic transformation strategy of solid inorganic−organic hybrids could open up significant technological opportunities for the preparation of advanced nanomaterials with fascinating performance in the near future.
